Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy
Cardiovascular
Exercise-induced palpitations/syncope, Epsilon waves on ECG, Family history of sudden death, RV fibrofatty replacement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-004
Reason for Visit: Episode of near-syncope and rapid palpitations immediately following physical exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a young adult experiencing exercise-induced palpitations and presyncope, with a concerning family history of sudden cardiac death. This presentation carries an immediate, high risk for lethal ventricular arrhythmias. Urgent in-person evaluation was unequivocally required to assess hemodynamic stability, rule out acute deterioration, and plan immediate investigation, satisfying the requirement that assessment could not wait until routine in-hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was going for a run and my heart suddenly started racing uncontrollably, my vision went dark, and I had to sit down before I passed out."
History of Present Illness: Patient was jogging when they experienced sudden, rapid palpitations associated with profound lightheadedness and presyncope. Symptoms self-resolved after 15 minutes of rest but have left the patient feeling lethargic and anxious. Worsening of ventricular arrhythmias during physical exertion noted over the last two months. Patient reports a family history of sudden unexplained cardiac death in a maternal uncle at age 30. KEY SYMPTOMS: Palpitations typically associated with exercise. Exertional syncope or presyncope. Family history of sudden unexplained cardiac death. Worsening of ventricular arrhythmias during physical exertion.
Functional Status: Highly active athlete, currently resting and fearful of mobilization.
Social/Safety Concerns: Psychological distress regarding family history.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Young, fit appearance, currently anxious but in no acute cardiopulmonary distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Rhythm is regular at rest, though frequent premature ventricular contractions are palpable. Right ventricular heave is present on palpation of the left parasternal edge. Jugular venous pressure is mildly elevated. No peripheral pitting edema or hepatomegaly noted at this stage. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Exercise-induced palpitations/syncope, Epsilon waves on ECG (presumed, pending test), Family history of sudden death, RV fibrofatty replacement (pending MRI).
Environmental Observations: Safe, stable home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Denies illicit drug use or performance-enhancing substances.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy - Status: Exacerbated (Symptomatic)
Differentials:

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear arrhythmic prodrome (palpitations) during exertion and highly significant family history of sudden cardiac death.

Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of an ejection systolic murmur radiating to the axilla/carotids and specific right-sided signs.

Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct correlation with physical exertion, signs of right ventricular overload, and concerning family history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient presented with exercise-induced palpitations and presyncope set against a critical family history of sudden cardiac death. These are red-flag indicators for an underlying inherited cardiomyopathy, specifically posing an immediate risk of fatal ventricular tachyarrhythmia. Deferring clinical assessment until the next routine appointment was absolutely contraindicated due to the risk of sudden cardiac arrest. Urgent home physical evaluation was mandatory to ensure current hemodynamic stability and facilitate an urgent, structured diagnostic pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Strict prohibition of all strenuous physical activity and competitive sports immediately. Referral generated for urgent resting 12-lead electrocardiogram, Holter monitor, and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging.
Positive Behavioral Change: Patient instructed to adopt a strict low-intensity daily routine. By voluntarily checking their pulse during mild activities and acknowledging a calm rate, the patient reinforces the safety of a restricted regimen. The reward is a sense of control and reduced anxiety regarding sudden arrhythmic events.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Expedited Cardiology referral. General Practice review within 48 hours to secure immediate ECG.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If palpitations recur while resting, if a syncopal episode occurs, or if severe chest pain develops, parents are instructed to call an ambulance immediately and commence basic life support if consciousness is lost.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal electrocardiographic and imaging investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Inherited cardiomyopathies require lifelong monitoring and restriction of high-level athletic activity. Definitive diagnosis drives specific management such as beta-blockade or implantable devices.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not engage in any running, heavy lifting, or sports. If your heart starts racing again while resting, call for an ambulance immediately.

